{"schemaVersion":"1.0.0","kind":"eigenquestions-fine-index","destinations":[{"id":"source-question:6473e6def09a2177dc59fdeeca4f20e03cb263df30cb2963ae8c603615ff827a","title":"Can a supersymmetric Lorentz quotient still exhibit a collapsing circle? The parabolic quotient produces a pinching geometry in which lightcone time also controls the circle radius, giving a null crunch followed by a bang.","connectionCount":1,"detail":{"shardId":"destination-details-fine-0001"},"indexTemplate":"source_question"},{"id":"source-question:6494c46fde09dbcd5574995e67ee4cd1eff854a5a0f2da2a0d476447317d65ef","title":"Can radial D3-brane motion, coupled to a stabilized but evolving volume modulus, yield enough inflation and acceptable primordial spectra? The relevant tests are inflation duration, scalar amplitude, scalar tilt, and tensor amplitude within the model and observational conventions supplied by the paper.","connectionCount":1,"detail":{"shardId":"destination-details-fine-0002"},"indexTemplate":"source_question"},{"id":"source-question:643e08b7f5423c7c7b7c5f3fc1a25d764be2a8c404889d5061d0aa5af9d814be","title":"When can heavy transverse fields leave observable primordial-spectrum features through sufficiently rapid turns of an inflationary trajectory, while slow roll continues, and when can a single-mode effective theory capture these effects?","connectionCount":1,"detail":{"shardId":"destination-details-fine-0010"},"indexTemplate":"source_question"},{"id":"source-question:647df82a6f86a7caa029795f1222e7a4577b78c3b592b5599df48c47e59013e7","title":"Given a boundary subregion density matrix ρ_A in a state with a classical geometric AdS/CFT dual, what is the largest bulk region fully reconstructible from it, equivalently common to all geometric duals compatible with that density matrix?","connectionCount":1,"detail":{"shardId":"destination-details-fine-0012"},"indexTemplate":"source_question"},{"id":"source-question:64a393aa6492efd9156dea63c8fe87c027cf894b6336cc1a4ce433488b91ca53","t